Sec. 50101 repeals Inflation Reduction Act provisions on onshore oil and gas royalty rates and noncompetitive leasing, restoring prior law. It directs the Secretary of the Interior to immediately resume quarterly onshore lease sales under the Mineral Leasing Act, with expedited scoping, comment, and NEPA review, and defines "eligible lands" broadly as all leasable lands not statutorily excluded, including those nominated, subject to drainage, or designated available by regulation. It requires at least 4 lease sales per fiscal year in each of Wyoming, New Mexico, Colorado, Utah, Montana, North Dakota, Oklahoma, Nevada, and Alaska, offering at least 50 percent of nominated available parcels and not restricting offers to one BLM field office unless all parcels are there. It mandates replacement sales if a sale is canceled, delayed, or deferred, or if 25 percent or more of offered acreage receives no bid, and amends the Mineral Leasing Act to require leasing within 18 months of an expression of interest, bar lease terms beyond the approved resource management plan, limit permits to drill to a single non-renewable 4-year term, and establish commingling approval standards with plus or minus 2 percent volume measurement uncertainty.

Sec. 50102 requires the Secretary of the Interior to conduct at least 30 region-wide oil and gas lease sales in the Gulf of America region (at least 1 by December 15, 2025, at least 2 per year in 2026–2039, and 1 by March 15, 2040) and at least 6 lease sales in Alaska's Cook Inlet Planning Area (2026–2028 and 2030–2032, by March 15), notwithstanding the 2024–2029 leasing program and except in existing moratoria areas. Gulf sales must use Lease Sale 254 terms and stipulations 4–9, may update stipulations 1–3 and 10, set royalties between 12.5 and 16.667 percent, and give 10-year primary terms in water 800 meters or deeper; Cook Inlet sales must use Lease Sale 244 terms, with 70 percent of revenues to Alaska beginning FY 2034. Each Gulf sale must offer at least 80,000,000 acres (or all available), each Alaska sale at least 1,000,000 acres (or all available), and the Secretary must approve offshore commingling unless it cannot be done safely or would reduce ultimate recovery. It repeals IRA Section 50261, caps OCS royalty rates at 16.667 percent, and raises Gulf of Mexico Energy Security Act distributed revenue limits to $650,000,000 annually for FY 2025–2034 and $500,000,000 annually for FY 2035–2055.

Sec. 50103 repeals Section 50263 of Public Law 117-169 (30 U.S.C. 1727), eliminating the Inflation Reduction Act's royalty on methane extracted from Federal and Indian lands.

Sec. 50104 requires the Secretary of the Interior, acting through BLM, to conduct at least 4 area-wide oil and gas lease sales in the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge Coastal Plain within 10 years, offering at least 400,000 acres per sale and the areas with highest hydrocarbon potential, on a schedule of 1 year, 3 years, 5 years, and 7 years after enactment. Sales must use the same terms and conditions as the 2020 Coastal Plain record of decision, and rights-of-way and surface development provisions of Public Law 115-97 apply. Of adjusted bonus, rental, and royalty receipts, 50 percent goes to Alaska for FY 2025–2033 and 70 percent for FY 2034 and thereafter, with the balance to Treasury miscellaneous receipts.

Sec. 50105 restores and resumes the National Petroleum Reserve-Alaska competitive oil and gas leasing program under the June 2020 NPR-A final environmental impact statement and December 2020 record of decision (excluding the September 20, 2022 errata sheet). The Secretary must conduct at least 5 lease sales within 10 years, offering at least 4,000,000 acres per sale, with the first within 1 year and additional sales every 2 years, using the same lease form, terms, and stipulations as those documents. Beginning FY 2034, 70 percent of receipts from sales, rentals, bonuses, and royalties go to Alaska and 30 percent to the Treasury.

Sec. 50201 requires the Secretary of the Interior, within 90 days of enactment, to publish required environmental review for each qualified coal lease application, establish fair market value, hold a lease sale, and identify the highest bidder at or above fair market value, granting the application; a qualified application is one pending on enactment or submitted within 90 days thereafter for which environmental review has commenced or can commence within 90 days. The Secretary may also grant additional approvals for previously issued coal leases to begin mining and issue the lease to the winning bidder after completing those steps.

Sec. 50202 amends Section 7(a) of the Mineral Leasing Act to set the coal royalty at 12.5 percent, except not more than 7 percent from enactment through September 30, 2034. The amendment applies to existing, non-terminated coal leases issued under Section 2 of the Mineral Leasing Act before, on, or after enactment. For lessees who paid advance royalties under Section 7(b), the Secretary must credit the difference between amounts paid before enactment and what would have been owed had the 7 percent rate applied earlier.

Sec. 50203 directs the Secretary of the Interior, within 90 days of enactment, to make available for lease not less than 4,000,000 additional acres of known recoverable coal resources on Federal land in the 48 contiguous States and Alaska, notwithstanding Section 2(a)(3)(A) of the Mineral Leasing Act and Section 202(a) of FLPMA. The acreage may not include National Monuments, National Recreation Areas, National Wilderness Preservation System components, Wild and Scenic Rivers System components, National Trails System components, National Conservation Areas, National Wildlife Refuge System units, National Fish Hatchery System units, or National Park System units.

Sec. 50204 authorizes mining of Federal coal reserves on Federal land subject to a mining plan approved by the Secretary of the Interior as of enactment and adjacent to coal reserves in adjacent State or private land, where the coal could not otherwise be mined economically. Within 90 days of enactment, the Secretary must take necessary steps, without substantial modification, to authorize mining of that Federal land. NEPA review is not prevented by this section.

Sec. 50301 requires the Forest Service to sell timber annually on National Forest System land in fiscal years 2026–2034 in quantities at least 250,000,000 board-feet greater than the prior fiscal year, subject to forest plan maximum allowable sale quantities, and to enter into at least 40 long-term timber sale contracts of at least 20 years during FY 2025–2034, with receipts to the Treasury general fund. It also requires BLM to sell timber annually on public lands in FY 2026–2034 in quantities at least 20,000,000 board-feet greater than the prior year, subject to applicable resource management plans, and to enter into at least 5 long-term contracts of at least 20 years for disposal of vegetative materials under the Materials Act of 1947, with receipts to the Treasury general fund.

Sec. 50302 establishes acreage rent for wind and solar rights-of-way on Federal land using the formula A × B × ((1 + C)^D), where A is the 5-year average per-acre pastureland rental rate for the State, B is 100 percent for solar and at least 10 percent for wind, C is 3 percent, and D is the year of the right-of-way term, collected annually beginning January 1 and paid until generation begins. It also imposes an annual capacity fee equal to the greater of the acreage rent or 3.9 percent of gross electricity sale proceeds, allows a 10 percent multiple-use reduction for wind projects where at least 25 percent of right-of-way land is authorized for other uses all year, and bars refunds for late-approved reductions. The Secretary may charge a late fee after 15 days and terminate a right-of-way after 90 days of nonpayment.

Sec. 50303 provides that, beginning January 1, 2026, amounts collected from renewable energy projects as bonus bids, rentals, fees, or other payments under rights-of-way, permits, leases, or other authorizations are deposited in the Treasury general fund and, without further appropriation, 25 percent is paid to the State where revenue is derived and 25 percent to each county based on the percentage of county land from which revenue is derived. Payments must be used consistent with Section 35 of the Mineral Leasing Act, are in addition to payments in lieu of taxes under chapter 69 of title 31, and are made available in the fiscal year immediately following collection.

Sec. 50304 rescinds the unobligated balances of amounts made available by Sections 50221, 50222, and 50223 of Public Law 117-169 (the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022), which provided National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management funds.

Sec. 50305 appropriates $150,000,000 to the Secretary of the Interior, acting through the Director of the National Park Service, for fiscal year 2025, available through fiscal year 2028, for events, celebrations, and activities commemorating the 250th anniversary of the founding of the United States.

Sec. 50401 appropriates $218,000,000 for maintenance and repairs of Strategic Petroleum Reserve storage and related facilities and $171,000,000 to purchase petroleum products for storage in the Reserve, available through September 30, 2029. It repeals Section 20003 of Public Law 115-97, the Strategic Petroleum Reserve drawdown and sale mandate.

Sec. 50402 repeals Section 50142 of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022 and rescinds its unobligated balance, and rescinds unobligated balances under Sections 50123, 50141, 50144, 50145, 50151, 50152, 50153, and 50161 of that law.

Sec. 50403 amends Section 1706 of the Energy Policy Act of 2005 to expand eligible loan guarantee purposes to increasing capacity or output and supporting known or forecastable electric supply for grid reliability or system adequacy, and to cover enabling identification, leasing, development, production, processing, transportation, transmission, refining, and generation of energy and critical minerals. It strikes subsection (c), redesignates subsections, and appropriates $1,000,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2028, with no more than 3 percent for administrative expenses. It also extends Section 50144(b) commitment authority of the Inflation Reduction Act from 2026 to 2028.

Sec. 50404 directs the Secretary of Energy to mobilize National Laboratories to partner with U.S. industry to curate and structure Department of Energy scientific data across the National Laboratory complex for artificial intelligence and machine learning use, and to initiate seed efforts for self-improving AI models for science and engineering. The curated data may be used to rapidly develop next-generation microelectronics with greater capabilities beyond Moore's law and lower energy consumption, and the AI models must be provided to the scientific community through the American science cloud to accelerate innovation for new energy technologies. It appropriates $150,000,000, available through September 30, 2026, to carry out the section.

Sec. 50501 appropriates $1,000,000,000 to the Secretary of the Interior, acting through the Commissioner of Reclamation, for fiscal year 2025 through September 30, 2034, for construction and associated activities restoring or increasing capacity or use of existing Bureau of Reclamation conveyance facilities or increasing capacity of existing Bureau of Reclamation surface water storage facilities, as determined by the Secretary. Contracts or agreements under the section are not treated as new or amended contracts for purposes of Section 203 of the Reclamation Reform Act of 1982 or Section 3404(a) of the Reclamation Projects Authorization and Adjustment Act of 1992. None of the funds are reimbursable or subject to matching or cost-sharing requirements. [end of text]


